    "outcome": "completed",
    "text": "I read both assigned sources in full through `evidence.read_evidence`: `evidence_qa078_operations` and `evidence_qa078_test_evidence`.\n\n**Findings**\n\n`evidence_qa078_operations` confirms a recovery blocker in `dispatch`: it updates `outbox.sentAt` before awaiting `broker.publish`. The operations contract says publish may throw before delivery, and the dispatcher contract says there is no separate scan/reconciler for queued jobs whose outbox is already marked sent. Concrete consequence: if an unsent outbox row is selected, `sentAt` is written, then `broker.publish({ eventId, jobId })` throws before delivery, restart will not retry that row because it is no longer `sentAt IS NULL`; the job can remain queued forever. Safe repair: publish first and only mark sent after durable broker acceptance, or introduce an explicit publish state/attempt table where “publishing” rows are recovered after crash. Regression should simulate pre-delivery throw and assert the event is retried after restart.\n\n`evidence_qa078_operations` also confirms an object-store race despite database fencing. `work` writes every attempt to the same key: `tenants/${job.tenantId}/exports/${job.id}.csv`; if `finish` fails, it deletes that same key. Concrete interleaving: worker A claims epoch 1 and pauses; lease expires; worker B claims epoch 2, renders, `put`s `job.id.csv`, and `finish` succeeds; worker A resumes, renders stale bytes, `put`s over the same key, `finish` fails due to DB fence, then deletes the shared key. The ready job can point to missing or stale bytes. Safe repair: each attempt writes a unique attempt-scoped object key, `finish` publishes only the winning key under the existing state/epoch/lease fence, and failed/stale attempts delete only their own attempt key. Regression should pause old worker, complete new worker, resume old worker, then assert the ready download still returns the new worker’s bytes and cleanup did not remove them.\n\nSecurity evidence is incomplete for revocation. `evidence_qa078_test_evidence` shows normal ready download, expiry rejection, tenant route isolation, forged body tenant ignored, and canceled-state download denial through calls to `authorizedRoute`, `issueLink`, and `download`. But the source for `security.mjs` is not in my assigned manifest, so I cannot confirm whether a previously issued, unexpired link is denied after membership revocation. Required test: issue a link while the user is a member, remove `memberKey(ctx)` from `members`, call `download` before expiry, and expect denial if the security contract requires current membership at download time. The fix, if absent, is to re-check current tenant membership during download while preserving existing tenant/signature/expiry validation.\n\nAPI idempotency evidence is also incomplete. The local checks confirm tenant-local uniqueness for the same payload and same key across tenants: forged body tenant does not override verified session tenant, same normalized payload replays to the same job, and another tenant gets a different job. However, `api-storage.mjs` is not in my assigned manifest, so I cannot determine whether same request key with changed payload is rejected or incorrectly returns the old job. Required regression: create with `requestKey=request-1, filter=paid`, then replay same tenant/key with materially changed payload, such as `filter=unpaid`, and expect a conflict/error rather than silently returning or creating an ambiguous job.\n\n**Confirmed Behavior To Preserve**\n\nThe assigned evidence supports preserving these existing protections: verified-session tenant boundary and forged body tenant rejection; tenant-local idempotency across tenants; atomic job/outbox rollback in the local check; database state/epoch/lease fencing implied by stale `finish` returning false; ready-job duplicate work returning `ignored`; canceled jobs denying download and duplicate reclaim.\n\n**Regression Plan**\n\nAdd concrete tests for: changed payload under same tenant/key; same key across tenants plus forged body tenant; job/outbox rollback; pre-delivery publish failure, lost acknowledgement after delivery, and duplicate delivery; old/new worker object publication and cleanup race; cancellation while work is paused; unexpired link after membership revocation; normal ready download, expiry, and ready duplicate no-op. Existing `evidence_qa078_test_evidence` already covers parts of tenant isolation, rollback, happy path, expiry, cancellation denial, and ready duplicate, but not the critical broker failure and object-store race cases.\n\n**Unresolved Evidence**\n\nNo production remote-store/IAM/consistency/durability configuration is provided; `evidence_qa078_operations` explicitly says the object store is an in-memory fixture only. Resolving this needs real storage configuration, IAM policy, consistency/versioning semantics, and durability guarantees.\n\nNo production load, lease-latency distribution, or measured recovery-time evidence is provided; `evidence_qa078_test_evidence` is local in-memory scripted checking only. Resolving this needs production-like load tests and recovery measurements under dispatcher restart, worker lease expiry, broker retry, and duplicate delivery.\n\n**Release Recommendation**\n\nDo not treat this as ready for release based on the assigned evidence. The dispatcher pre-delivery loss and stale-worker object overwrite/delete race are confirmed blockers. Repair recovery ordering first, then make object writes attempt-scoped, then add the regression cases above. Security revocation and changed-payload idempotency remain evidence gaps until `security.mjs` and `api-storage.mjs` or equivalent tests are reviewed.",
