    "text": "Based only on the two assigned sources read in full with `evidence.read_evidence`: `evidence_qa078_api_storage` rev `789cfd5...` and `evidence_qa078_security` rev `e309bb4...`.\n\n**Findings**\n\n`evidence_qa078_api_storage` has a tenant-local idempotency defect in `createExport`. The contract says same tenant plus same `requestKey` with a different normalized payload must be rejected as a conflict. The implementation computes `payloadHash`, but on replay it only does `SELECT * FROM jobs WHERE tenantId=? AND requestKey=?` and immediately `return existing`. Concrete input: tenant `t1`, request key `k`, first body `{format:\"csv\", filter:\"status=open\"}`, second body `{format:\"csv\", filter:\"status=closed\"}`. Observed consequence: the second request is accepted as a replay of the old job instead of conflicting, so callers can believe a changed export was accepted while receiving the prior job. Safe repair: when `existing` is found, compare `existing.payloadHash` to the newly computed hash; return existing only on match, otherwise throw a conflict error without writing a new job or changing stored data.\n\n`evidence_qa078_security` violates the download membership contract. `issueLink` checks current membership through `requireMember`, but `download` only verifies the token signature, expiry, job id, tenant id, and ready state. It never checks that `members` still contains the signed `{tenantId,userId}`. Concrete consequence: a user can issue a valid unexpired link, have membership revoked, and still download until expiry. Safe repair: after `decode`, check active membership for `value.tenantId/value.userId` before reading the object. Preserve the existing signature, expiry, tenant id, and ready-state checks.\n\nThere is also a cancellation/download gap visible across the two sources. The API contract says cancellation invalidates future downloads, including ready jobs. `cancelExport` can set a ready job to `canceled`, and `download` denies anything not currently `ready`, so that part is preserved. But previously issued unexpired links remain cryptographically valid; the denial depends entirely on the DB state and current membership check. The missing membership check is the confirmed blocker here.\n\nFor recovery, only partial evidence is present. `evidence_qa078_api_storage` does show job and outbox insertion in the same SQLite transaction, with the test hook `afterInsert()` before outbox insertion. If that hook throws, the transaction should roll back both the job and the missing outbox insert, preserving atomicity. However, no dispatcher code, broker publish order, sentAt update behavior, restart loop, or consumer deduplication implementation is included in my assigned sources. I cannot confirm behavior for pre-delivery publish failure, lost acknowledgement after delivery, restart, or duplicate delivery from these files. The safe plan should be: select unsent outbox events durably, publish before marking `sentAt`, leave `sentAt` null on publish failure, tolerate duplicate delivery when acknowledgement is lost, and make worker execution idempotent/fenced by job state/epoch rather than promising exactly-once external execution.\n\nFor concurrency, `claim` and `finish` preserve important DB fencing. `claim` only claims queued or expired running jobs, increments `epoch`, and sets a lease. `finish` publishes ready only when `id`, `tenantId`, `state='running'`, matching `epoch`, and unexpired `leaseUntil` all hold. That correctly prevents an old worker from updating the DB after lease expiry and a newer claim. The unresolved race is object-store side effects: `finish` receives an already-written `objectKey`, but there is no evidence of object write ordering or cleanup. Concrete interleaving: worker A claims epoch 1 and writes object A; its lease expires. Worker B claims epoch 2, writes object B, and successfully finishes DB to ready with B. Worker A later calls `finish` and is fenced out, but object A may remain orphaned, or if cleanup is implemented elsewhere without epoch/object ownership it could delete B. Safe repair: use epoch-scoped object keys, only publish DB with the matching epoch, and cleanup only the losing worker’s own epoch/object after failed `finish`; cancellation cleanup must also be fenced against current DB state/objectKey.\n\n**Confirmed Correct Behavior To Preserve**\n\nThe verified-session tenant boundary is present in `createExport`, `getOwned`, and membership-gated routes: body `tenantId` is ignored for creation because `ctx.tenantId` is used. Tenant-local uniqueness is preserved by `UNIQUE(tenantId, requestKey)`, so different tenants may reuse the same request key.\n\nThe accepted-job/outbox atomicity shape is present: job insert and outbox insert happen inside one `db.transaction`. Ready/canceled duplicate behavior is partly preserved: `claim` refuses ready and canceled jobs, and `finish` is a no-op unless the job is still running with the same epoch and live lease.\n\nThe download path preserves valid signature, expiry, tenant binding, and ready-state controls. Those should be retained while adding revocation enforcement.\n\n**Regression Plan**\n\nAdd a replay-conflict test: create a job for tenant `t1` with key `k` and filter `a`; replay with key `k` and filter `b`; expect conflict, one job row, one outbox row, and original payload unchanged.\n\nAdd tenant/body-forgery tests: tenant `t1` creates with body containing forged `tenantId:\"t2\"`; stored tenant must be `t1`. Then tenant `t2` uses the same request key and same or different filter; expect an independent job due to tenant-local uniqueness.\n\nAdd rollback atomicity test: call `createExport` with `afterInsert` throwing; expect no job row and no outbox row. Then call without throwing; expect both job and outbox row committed.\n\nAdd recovery tests around the dispatcher once that source is available: publish failure before delivery leaves `sentAt` null and republishes after restart; lost acknowledgement after delivery causes duplicate delivery; duplicate worker delivery must not create multiple ready publications or corrupt final state.\n\nAdd old/new worker race test: worker A claims and writes object A, lease expires, worker B claims and writes object B, B finishes successfully, A finish returns false. Expected DB objectKey is B; cleanup removes only A.\n\nAdd cancellation-during-work test: worker claims, cancellation increments epoch and sets state canceled, worker finish returns false, future claim returns null, and any worker-owned object is cleaned without deleting another epoch’s object.\n\nAdd revocation test: ready job issues link for user `u1`; remove `t1/u1` from members; download before expiry must fail with membership revoked, not return store bytes.\n\nAdd normal download tests: ready job with active membership downloads store object; expired link fails; duplicate ready-job delivery or duplicate `finish` leaves the job ready with one final objectKey and no second publication.\n\n**Release Recommendation**\n\nI would not recommend release from this evidence set. Confirmed blockers are the idempotency conflict bug and the unexpired-link-after-revocation authorization bug. Recovery and object-store cleanup behavior also remain evidence gaps because the dispatcher, broker integration, remote store/IAM/consistency/durability configuration, and production load/lease-latency/recovery-time measurements were not provided.\n\nFeasible order: fix idempotency hash comparison, add download-time membership enforcement, add the focused regression tests above, then review dispatcher/store sources for publish ordering, duplicate delivery, and object cleanup fencing. Production readiness also needs real deployment evidence for object store/IAM/durability and measured lease/recovery behavior; local scripted checks would not substitute for those production verifications.",
