    "text": "Read in full: `evidence_qa078_operations` at `b98c8d2d36be1d7173b0c3a2032ce8e87cd7b5fd` and `evidence_qa078_test_evidence` at `a33481c55b120c06dbe3571212bbc5fdf6cb6b18`.\n\nThe release is not ready based on the assigned evidence. Two confirmed blockers are visible in `evidence_qa078_operations`.\n\nFirst, dispatcher recovery is unsafe for broker failure before delivery. `dispatch` selects unsent outbox rows, immediately sets `sentAt`, then awaits `broker.publish` (`evidence_qa078_operations`). The operations contract says publish may throw before delivery or after delivery with lost acknowledgement, and that there is no separate reconciler for queued jobs whose outbox is already marked sent. Concrete consequence: if `publish({ eventId, jobId })` throws before delivery after `sentAt` is written, the outbox row is no longer selected on restart and no worker receives the job. The safe repair is to publish before permanently marking sent, or use a lease/in-flight publish state with retryable failure semantics; only record durable sent/acknowledged state after publish acceptance. Because lost acknowledgement after delivery can still cause retry, consumers must remain idempotent by stable `eventId`/`jobId`; do not promise exactly-once external execution.\n\nSecond, worker object publication has a confirmed stale-worker race. `work` renders, writes to `tenants/${tenantId}/exports/${job.id}.csv`, then calls `finish`; if `finish` fails it deletes that same key (`evidence_qa078_operations`). Since all workers for the same job use the same object key, a stale or canceled worker can overwrite the winning worker’s bytes before losing the DB fence, then delete the key after `finish` returns false. Concrete interleaving: worker A claims epoch 1 and pauses past lease; worker B claims epoch 2, writes `paid-export-v2`, `finish` succeeds and the job becomes ready; worker A resumes, writes `paid-export-v1` to the same key, `finish` fails because the DB fence rejects it, then deletes the shared key. The database fencing may be correct, but the object store side is not fenced. Repair by writing each attempt to a unique immutable attempt key, publishing that exact key only if `finish` wins, and deleting only the losing attempt’s private key. Ready job duplicates should remain no-ops.\n\nSecurity evidence is incomplete for the revocation criterion. The local scripted checks show a normal ready download succeeds, expiry is enforced, and canceled state denies download (`evidence_qa078_test_evidence`). They also show the worker render scope uses stored `{ tenantId: \"cobalt\", filter: \"paid\" }`, not an event-supplied tenant (`evidence_qa078_operations`; asserted in `evidence_qa078_test_evidence`). However, the assigned evidence does not include `security.mjs`, so I cannot verify the actual signature format, authorization checks, or the required case of a previously issued unexpired link after membership revocation. A regression must issue a link while the user is a member, remove `memberKey(ctx)` from `members`, then attempt `download` before expiry and expect denial while preserving valid tenant/signature/expiry controls.\n\nAPI evidence is also partial. The local checks confirm a forged body tenant is ignored in favor of the verified session tenant, same payload replay in the same tenant returns the same job, same request key in another tenant produces a different job, and `getOwned` denies cross-tenant access (`evidence_qa078_test_evidence`). They also confirm atomic rollback of job/outbox creation when an injected crash occurs: no rollback job remains and the outbox count is unchanged except for the two earlier successful jobs. But the assigned evidence does not include `api-storage.mjs`, so changed-payload replay with the same idempotency key is not directly verified here. A required test should submit the same tenant and request key with a materially different payload and expect either the original response only when the payload hash matches, or a conflict/error when it differs.\n\nRegression plan should include observable scenarios, not just names: same key changed payload conflict or documented equivalent; same key across tenants and forged body tenant remains scoped to verified session; injected transaction failure leaves neither job nor outbox row; publish failure before delivery is retried after restart, while lost acknowledgement after delivery may redeliver and worker idempotency prevents duplicate publication; old/new worker object race cannot overwrite or delete the winner; cancellation during paused work prevents DB publication and only cleans the paused attempt object; issued unexpired link after membership revocation is denied; normal ready download works, expiry fails, and duplicate ready job delivery is ignored.\n\nPreserved correct behavior from the assigned evidence: verified-session tenant boundary is honored in the local check, tenant-local uniqueness is observed, job/outbox creation rollback is observed, DB-side claim/finish fencing is implied by ready duplicate returning `ignored`, and ready/canceled duplicate worker delivery is a no-op in the scripted checks. These should be retained while fixing dispatcher ordering and object-store publication.\n\nUnresolved evidence needs remain exactly as stated by the local evidence: this is “local in-memory SQLite/object store and scripted broker, not production,” with untested remote storage/IAM/durability and production load/recovery-time distribution (`evidence_qa078_test_evidence`). The operations contract likewise says no production object-store contract, IAM policy, worker lease-latency distribution, load benchmark, or measured recovery-time evidence is provided (`evidence_qa078_operations`). Resolving those requires real store/IAM/consistency/durability configuration review plus measured production-like lease latency, load, restart, and recovery-time tests. No operational approval or execution is granted by this review.",
