    "text": "I read both assigned sources in full with `evidence.read_evidence`: `evidence_qa078_api_storage` and `evidence_qa078_security`.\n\n**Findings**\n\n1. Tenant-local idempotency accepts changed payload replay instead of conflicting. In `evidence_qa078_api_storage`, `createExport` computes `payloadHash` from normalized `{ format, filter }`, but on an existing `(tenantId, requestKey)` it immediately `return existing` without comparing `existing.payloadHash` to the new hash. Concrete input: same verified `ctx.tenantId`, same `requestKey`, first body `{ format: \"csv\", filter: \"status=open\" }`, replay body `{ format: \"csv\", filter: \"status=closed\" }`. Observed consequence: caller receives the original job, silently tied to the old filter, although the API contract says different normalized payload with that key must be rejected without new job or changed data. Safe repair: after loading `existing`, compare `existing.payloadHash` to the new `payloadHash`; return existing only on match, otherwise throw a conflict error and do not mutate.\n\n2. Download authorization does not enforce current membership after link issuance. `evidence_qa078_security` states every download must check current active membership for the signed user/tenant, including unexpired links after revocation. But `download` only decodes the signed token, checks job id/tenant and `state === \"ready\"`, then returns `store.get(job.objectKey)`. It never checks `members.has(\\`${value.tenantId}/${value.userId}\\`)`. Consequence: a user whose membership is revoked after link issuance can still download until expiry if they possess the valid token. Safe repair: after `decode`, require current membership for `value.tenantId/value.userId` before reading the store. Preserve the existing signature, expiry, tenant id, and ready-state checks.\n\n3. Recovery behavior is under-specified by the provided implementation. `createExport` inserts the job and outbox event in one transaction, and `afterInsert()` before outbox insert correctly models rollback: an injected exception should roll back the job as well. However, the assigned sources include no dispatcher implementation, no broker publish order, no sent marker update logic, and no consumer deduplication beyond `outbox.jobId UNIQUE`. Safe order should be: persist job plus unsent outbox atomically, publish only unsent outbox events after commit, mark sent only after confirmed broker acceptance, retry unsent rows on restart, and make worker/job handling idempotent under duplicate delivery. For lost acknowledgement after delivery, retry can duplicate the external broker message; the worker path must tolerate duplicate delivery and must not promise exactly-once external execution.\n\n4. Database publication is fenced, but object-store writes and cleanup are not covered. `claim` only claims queued or expired running jobs and increments `epoch`; `finish` publishes `ready` only when `state='running'`, matching `tenantId`, matching `epoch`, and unexpired lease. That is good DB fencing. A concrete remaining race: worker A claims epoch 1 and writes object `A`; its lease expires. Worker B claims epoch 2, writes object `B`, and `finish` succeeds. Worker A later calls `finish`; DB update fails due to epoch mismatch, but object `A` may remain in the store unless the worker deletes it after failed publish or writes only after a final valid claim. Cancellation has a similar gap: a canceled job prevents `finish` from updating DB, but a paused worker can still write bytes unless object writes are fenced/cleaned. Safe repair: use epoch-scoped temporary object keys, publish object key to DB only after fenced `finish` succeeds, and delete temp objects on failed finish/cancel/lease loss with a periodic sweeper for abandoned temps.\n\n**Confirmed Correct Behavior To Preserve**\n\nThe assigned sources do show several protections that should remain intact:\n\n- Verified session tenant is the authority for create/get/cancel; body tenant is ignored in `createExport`.\n- `UNIQUE(tenantId, requestKey)` gives tenant-local key uniqueness, so different tenants may reuse a request key.\n- Accepted job and outbox event are created in the same DB transaction.\n- `getOwned` enforces tenant-local reads for session routes.\n- `claim` and `finish` provide state, epoch, and lease fencing for DB publication.\n- Ready and canceled jobs cannot be claimed because `claim` only accepts `queued` or `running`.\n- Duplicate work finishing after ready/canceled is a no-op at the DB level because `finish` requires `state='running'` and matching epoch.\n\n**Regression Plan**\n\nAdd tests with concrete actions and observable results:\n\n- Same tenant, same key, changed normalized filter: first create succeeds; second create returns conflict; job count and outbox count remain unchanged.\n- Same key across tenants plus forged body tenant: tenant A and tenant B can each create with the same key; a body `tenantId` claiming another tenant does not affect stored `tenantId`.\n- Job/outbox rollback: make `afterInsert` throw; assert no job and no outbox row remain.\n- Dispatcher recovery: simulate publish failure before delivery; row remains unsent and restart republishes. Simulate lost ack after delivery; duplicate message may occur, but worker dedup/fencing leaves one ready DB publication.\n- Old/new worker race: worker A lease expires after writing temp object; worker B claims newer epoch and succeeds; A finish fails and A temp object is deleted or swept.\n- Cancellation during paused work: worker claims, job is canceled, worker resumes and writes/finishes; DB remains canceled and any temp object is removed.\n- Unexpired link after revocation: issue ready-job link, remove `${tenantId}/${userId}` from members, download before expiry fails with membership error.\n- Normal download behavior: active member can download a ready job; expired link fails; duplicate ready-job delivery/finish does not alter the ready object unexpectedly.\n\n**Unresolved Evidence Needs**\n\nNo real remote object-store/IAM/consistency/durability configuration is provided. That would require deployment/IAM policy, bucket/container settings, encryption/access controls, retention/lifecycle rules, and consistency guarantees.\n\nNo production load, lease-latency, broker recovery-time, or object cleanup timing measurements are provided. That would require observed metrics or load/recovery test results. The checks above are proposed regressions from local source behavior, not production verification.\n\n**Release Recommendation**\n\nDo not treat this as release-ready based on the assigned evidence. The idempotency conflict bug and revoked-member download bug are direct contract violations. Repair those first, then add dispatcher recovery and duplicate-delivery tests, then close the object-store fencing/cleanup race, and finally verify against real storage/IAM and production timing evidence. No code changes or operational approval were performed in this review.",
