    "kind": "plain_reply",
    "runId": "run_qa078_export_11",
    "role": "contributor2",
    "outcome": "completed",
    "text": "Based only on the two assigned fixed sources, I cannot make a full release-readiness determination for the whole service because the core API/storage and security implementations are not in my manifest. I did read both assigned sources in full: `evidence_qa078_operations` at `b98c8d2d...` and `evidence_qa078_test_evidence` at `a33481c...`.\n\n**Findings**\n\nThe dispatcher marks an outbox row sent before broker publication succeeds. In `evidence_qa078_operations`, `dispatch()` runs `UPDATE outbox SET sentAt=?` and only then awaits `broker.publish(...)`. The operations contract says publish may throw before delivery, while the dispatcher contract says there is no separate scan/reconciler for queued jobs whose outbox is already marked sent. Concrete consequence: if `broker.publish` throws before delivery, the event is never delivered, the outbox row is no longer selected by future dispatches, and the job can remain queued forever. Repair: publish before recording `sentAt`, or use a transactional/claim state such as `publishingAt` with retryable failure handling; only mark durable sent after acknowledged broker acceptance. Regression: inject a broker that throws before delivery, restart dispatcher, and assert the outbox row is retried and the job eventually reaches ready.\n\nThere is an object-store race between stale workers and the winning worker. In `work()`, every worker writes the same key, `tenants/${job.tenantId}/exports/${job.id}.csv`, before calling `finish()`. If worker A’s lease expires, worker B claims and writes winning bytes, then stale worker A later writes different bytes to the same key before failing `finish()`, A’s cleanup deletes that shared key. The operations contract explicitly requires stale/canceled workers to neither overwrite nor delete winning bytes. Existing DB fencing may still prevent stale DB publication, but it does not fence object writes because the object key is shared before `finish()`. Repair: write to attempt-scoped immutable keys, for example including claim epoch/lease token, publish only the winning key through fenced `finish()`, and delete only the failed attempt’s own key. Regression: pause old worker past lease, let new worker finish ready, resume old worker, and assert the ready job still downloads the new worker’s bytes and no stale cleanup removes them.\n\nThe local test evidence does not cover changed-payload idempotency. `evidence_qa078_test_evidence` checks same tenant/same key/same normalized payload replay by asserting the same id is returned, and same key across tenants by asserting a different id. It does not show behavior for same tenant/same request key with changed payload. If the API contract requires rejecting changed payload replay, add a regression with `requestKey: \"request-1\"` and a materially different `filter` or `format`, expecting a conflict/error and no new job/outbox mutation.\n\nThe assigned evidence does not confirm download authorization after membership revocation. The tests issue a link while membership exists and verify normal download and expiry, then cancellation denies download. They do not remove the member from `members` and retry an otherwise unexpired link. Given the security criterion, the required regression is: issue link, remove `memberKey(ctx)` from `members`, attempt `download(...)` before expiry, expect authorization failure. The preserved controls shown locally are signature/expiry behavior and ready-state enforcement, but revocation behavior is unresolved from my assigned sources.\n\n**Confirmed Correct Behavior From Assigned Evidence**\n\nThe worker render scope uses stored job data, not broker event tenant data: `work()` calls `render({ tenantId: job.tenantId, filter: job.filter })`. The local test confirms a forged body tenant is ignored at creation time and render receives `{ tenantId: \"cobalt\", filter: \"paid\" }`.\n\nThe local scripted checks confirm tenant-local uniqueness for same-payload replay and cross-tenant separation: same tenant/key/payload returns the same job id; another tenant using the same body gets a different job id; cross-tenant `getOwned` throws `not_found`.\n\nThe local scripted checks confirm job/outbox rollback for an injected crash during `createExport`: no `jobs` row with `requestKey='rollback'` remains and the existing outbox count stays at 2.\n\nThe local checks preserve ready duplicate behavior: after one successful `work()`, duplicate delivery of the same event returns `\"ignored\"` and render count remains 1. Cancellation also causes later work on the same event to return `\"ignored\"`.\n\n**Recovery Assessment**\n\nBroker lost acknowledgement after delivery is safer than pre-delivery failure under the current dispatcher order: because `sentAt` is already recorded before `publish`, a throw after actual delivery does not prevent the delivered event from being worked, but the dispatcher will not retry it. That still relies on broker redelivery/consumer completion and does not provide exactly-once external execution. A safer order is durable broker acceptance first, then mark sent; duplicate deliveries must remain harmless through `claim`/`finish` deduplication and attempt-scoped object writes.\n\nThe evidence explicitly states consumers are at-least-once and possibly reordered, and workers can pause past the lease. The correct target is idempotent, fenced, retryable processing, not exactly-once execution.\n\n**Unresolved Evidence Needs**\n\nNo production object-store/IAM/consistency/durability configuration is provided. The `memoryStore()` fixture says `put` replaces bytes, `get` returns current bytes, and `delete` removes the key, but the operations contract says this is not proof of real remote-store behavior. Resolving this requires production storage configuration, IAM policy, consistency/versioning/durability guarantees, and failure-mode tests against the real store or a faithful integration environment.\n\nNo production load, lease-latency, or recovery-time measurements are provided. The test evidence is explicitly “local in-memory SQLite/object store and scripted broker, not production.” Resolving this requires measured worker lease duration distributions, dispatcher restart timing, broker redelivery timing, recovery-time observations, and load tests under representative concurrency.\n\n**Release Recommendation**\n\nFrom these assigned sources, release should be blocked until at least the dispatcher pre-delivery loss and object-store stale-worker overwrite/delete race are repaired and covered by regressions. In parallel, add the missing idempotency, membership-revocation, broker failure, cancellation-during-paused-work, and normal download/expiry/duplicate tests. This review did not make code changes or grant operational approval.",
